WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.740
- I remember watching Magic School Bus

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.140
in school. Do you remember?
Is it medically accurate?

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.240
We're about to find out, be-woo!

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.530
- What are you doing?

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.680
- Our class is broadcasting
two hours of live television

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.370
today, and I said I'd have a show

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:16.195
for them to do, and I don't, yet.

00:00:16.195 --> 00:00:17.290
(coughing)

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.710
- That's how I feel making
every YouTube video.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.750
And cover your mouth when
you're coughing, buddy.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.700
- It's my opinion as your
mother and as a doctor,

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.760
that you have a fever,
which means you are sick,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.380
which means you are going back to bed.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.220
- How did she know he has a fever?

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.010
She did not measure his temperature.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.820
She did not feel his forehead.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.480
- Yes, Dr. Tennelli,
thank you for calling.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.450
- What is this stegosaurus
that is on top of her shoulder?

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.570
- Is Ralphie staying home from
school today, Ms. Frizzle?

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.730
- I can't overstate
the importance of this,

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.380
do not send your kids to
school if they are sick.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.870
Then it prevents the other
kids from getting sick, too.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.480
- How can anything that smells

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.360
like grape shoe polish
help my body get well?

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.310
- Spoiler alert, it doesn't.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:00.410
Cough syrup doesn't really
help you get better.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:01.900
And in fact, when we
look at the research at

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.000
over-the-counter cough syrups,
they don't work that well.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.330
Just use some magical honey,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.410
as long as you're over the age of one.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.380
Why am I saying it to you as if you're one

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.909
and you understand what I'm saying?

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:11.742
(beeping)

00:01:11.742 --> 00:01:12.575
- [Dr. Tennelli] And don't forget

00:01:12.575 --> 00:01:14.230
to shake well before using.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:15.313
- Like this?

00:01:16.281 --> 00:01:18.870
- (laughs) Even your jokes are sick.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:19.703
- Wow.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.870
Wow. Standup comedy?

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.280
- [Dr. Tennelli] I've gotta
go see another patient,

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.440
but grandpa's downstairs
if you need anything.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.590
- Okay, Mama Bear, you're a doctor.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.580
You know that if you're
kissing your son who is sick,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.510
you're potentially getting yourself sick,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.210
thereby getting your patient sick.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.360
Don't do that.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.082
- Good afternoon, I'm Ralphie,

00:01:39.082 --> 00:01:41.140
and this is the Frizzle News Network.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.363
- I need to know what
this brontosaurus is.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.125
(screaming)

00:01:47.125 --> 00:01:49.929
(upbeat music)

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:52.639
(suspenseful music)

00:01:52.639 --> 00:01:53.632
- [Dorothy Ann] The FNN News Team

00:01:53.632 --> 00:01:55.100
is nearing the disaster area.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.370
- Okay, so they're in
the oral pharynx here.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.290
They're by the uvula,
that little punching bag

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.620
that hangs from the back of your throat.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.840
It looks like they're going
low to check out the tonsils,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.720
the pharynx, maybe even the
larynx if they go low enough.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.470
- Oh, get a shot of those
vocal chords, class!

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.750
- [Tim] Look how red and
swollen his throat is.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.240
- Most kids when they have a sore throat,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.140
it's not their vocal chords
that are the problem.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.240
It's usually higher up.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.320
Tonsils, which is why
you hear tonsillitis,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.070
or pharyngitis, which is just
your typical sore throat.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.300
But that is higher than the
larynx, so I don't know why

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.170
they ventured all the
way down to the larynx.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.520
- It's not as easy for us or germs

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.940
to get inside the body as you might think.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.360
- Yeah, skin pretty much covers it.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.690
- Yo, what is this pterodactyl
that's just in every scene?

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.300
- I've got it!

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.243
- The nose!

00:02:40.204 --> 00:02:42.810
(suspenseful music)

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:43.643
- Look at this!

00:02:43.643 --> 00:02:47.333
Is it just me or does
this say, this way in?

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.810
- How are you gonna get into the mouth

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.680
through a scab on your thigh?

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.270
That is one journey the Magic School Bus

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.580
probably won't make.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:58.912
- We're all gonna die!

00:02:58.912 --> 00:02:59.745
(digital beeping)

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:02.820
- It's a shortcut, get it? Shortcut?

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.740
- [All] Carlos!

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.989
- I love puns. Carlos, not a good pun.

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:07.822
- Wow!

00:03:07.822 --> 00:03:09.820
- Dude, what is this triceratops
doing? Someone tell me.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.840
- For every trip, there's a roadmap.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.650
- [Girl] That's Ralphie's bloodstream.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.094
- Good bloodstream.

00:03:16.094 --> 00:03:18.610
♪ We'll take the
trans-Ralphie highway system ♪

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:22.440
And here we go, wa-hoo!

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.370
- What doesn't make sense to me is,

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.940
let's say they take the venous
system back up to his throat.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.700
The venous system doesn't
empty at his throat,

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.830
it empties into the heart.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.330
In order to get to the
throat, they need to take

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.840
the venous system back to
the heart, from the heart,

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.300
back up to the throat, and
then from the capillaries,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.420
kind of enter the throat?

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.386
Diffusion?

00:03:38.386 --> 00:03:39.333
(TV static)

00:03:39.333 --> 00:03:40.470
- And it doesn't even hurt!

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:41.570
- This kid's not panicking?

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:44.001
There's a velociraptor
sitting next to him.

00:03:44.001 --> 00:03:45.400
(adventurous music)

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.130
They ventured into a capillary.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.315
Those are red blood cells floating around.

00:03:49.315 --> 00:03:50.148
(children yelling)

00:03:50.148 --> 00:03:52.630
Platelets, white blood cells
floating in there as well.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:53.890
- [Ralphie] That stuff is clear.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.980
- [Ms. Frizzle] That's right, Ralphie.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.100
The liquid part part
of the blood is clear.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.210
- It's actually not clear,
it's yellow. It's plasma.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.740
We talked about this. If you haven't,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.160
video linked down below.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:04.580
- Are they what make blood-

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.220
- What is this ankylosaurus doing

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:08.270
watching television and filming?

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.530
- They're called red blood cells,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.747
and the white ones are white blood cells.

00:04:13.747 --> 00:04:15.800
- [Ralphie] But what are
those jaggedy things?

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.320
- Platelets. - [Ms. Frizzle]
Those are platelets.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.550
- [Dorothy Ann] They help the
body heal scrapes and cuts.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.440
- That's true, blood has
the four components there,

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.280
platelets, white blood cells,
red blood cells, and plasma.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.130
Plasma makes up 55% of your blood,

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.330
and it's yellow, so your
blood's really yellow.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:30.420
- Bacteria are germs.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.720
Once inside our bodies,
they can make us sick.

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:36.040
Ralphie has a bacterial infection!

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.380
- What a great diagnosis,
and that's absolutely true,

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:39.840
bacterial infections do make us sick.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:40.880
They can give us sore throats,

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.610
that's why you hear the term strep throat.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:43.960
Sore throats, the majority of time,

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.790
aren't caused by bacterial
infections, are caused

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.010
by viruses, therefore,
not requiring antibiotics.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.225
Such an important point.

00:04:50.225 --> 00:04:52.180
- Look, here comes a white blood cell.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.977
It's throwing stuff at the bacteria.

00:04:56.926 --> 00:04:58.430
- Basically what happens is the bacteria

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.420
gets labeled as foreign.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.530
It starts getting attacked and eaten alive

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:03.660
by the macrophages.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.070
The neutrophils start
multiplying in number.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:07.710
And doing an inflammatory cascade,

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.240
you actually call on
more white blood cells

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.040
to come on and beat up on this bacteria.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.050
But then remember, when you're
having this inflammatory

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.760
cascade, it's also causing
damage to the surrounding tissue.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.110
The way I like to explain this
is imagine a war just went on

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.720
on some really beautiful grass,

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.350
and even though the good guys won,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.030
and they're all safe and sound,

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.190
the grass got pretty torn
up. That's your throat.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:29.190
That's what needs time to
heal. That's why it hurts.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.370
- No wonder I'm sick.
- There's a spinosaurus

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:32.840
hiding underneath his blanket.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.180
- I'm going to try and
get an exclusive interview

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:36.280
with these two bacteria.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.640
- If you ever run into a
bacteria, do not interview it.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:40.857
They will infect you.

00:05:41.837 --> 00:05:42.813
Infect you.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.109
I just saw an iguanodon pop up.

00:05:46.109 --> 00:05:48.320
(cheerful music)

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.360
The plesiosaur just
turned on the television!

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.020
- [Ralphie] Is that the
medicine mom gave me earlier?

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:54.140
- [Ms. Frizzle] It sure is.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:55.640
- Okay, take back what
I said, it looks like

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.240
the mom slash doctor
gave him an antibiotic.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.070
How did she know it was bacterial?

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.210
I don't know because she
didn't do a strep test.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:03.660
She didn't do a throat culture.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.320
I feel like this is encouraging
the overuse of antibiotics,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.080
but maybe I'm taking it too seriously

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.570
because it's a cartoon.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.640
- Does it mean what I think it means?

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.640
Oh no, Ralphie's antibodies
have marked the bus as bacteria.

00:06:17.723 --> 00:06:20.423
- The macrophage is about
to eat the magic school bus.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.730
Why isn't the apatosaurus
waking Ralphie up

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:24.720
so he can save them?

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.582
- Ralphie's white blood cells
will have to catch us first.

00:06:28.582 --> 00:06:31.472
(engine revving)

00:06:31.472 --> 00:06:34.472
(adventurous music)

00:06:35.849 --> 00:06:38.380
- It's a good thing bacteria
don't have hovercraft engines

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:42.093
or turbines on the back of them
to escape white blood cells.

00:06:43.097 --> 00:06:44.060
(alarm ringing)

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.930
I've never seen a pteranodon
use an alarm clock before.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:48.986
This is certainly a first.

00:06:48.986 --> 00:06:51.902
(sneaky music)

00:06:51.902 --> 00:06:52.870
(gong resonating)

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.240
This brachiosaurus is
really getting witty.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.660
- We left Ralphie's throat

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.790
and are now heading up his
nasal pharyngeal passages.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:00.700
- Prediction time.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.080
They're gonna get clumped
in a piece of mucus

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.290
and they're gonna get sneezed
right out. Final answer.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:07.213
- We're up Ralphie's nose?

00:07:09.106 --> 00:07:10.580
- [All] Ew!

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:12.870
- They're like, throat, cool, nose, ew!

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.253
What's the difference?

00:07:15.443 --> 00:07:16.276
(sneezing)

00:07:16.276 --> 00:07:17.740
Why is the mosasaurus sneezing?

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.610
Can you get inter-species
infectivity like this?

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.440
- I get it!

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.263
I've gotta sneeze them out,
but I don't feel like sneezing.

00:07:30.415 --> 00:07:32.665
(sneezing)

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.393
- This diplodocus is one smart dino.

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.490
- [Doctor] Sorry, Liz,
but I can't hear a thing.

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:45.360
- Do dilophosauruses even have lungs?

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.060
- Getting all your shots is
another way to keep healthy.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:49.930
Shots help your body be prepared

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:51.780
for some of the really tough germs.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.780
- A vaccine is like a wanted poster

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.070
where you essentially tell
all your cells to be ready

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.520
for this foreign invader
in case it does come along.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:00.400
- [Girl] Hey, does Ms.
Frizzle ever get sick?

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:02.010
- No way!

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.920
She takes good care of herself,

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.223
gets all of her shots, and she's magic.

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:10.380
- I don't think that's an
accurate representation to kids.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:11.750
It's okay to get sick.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:14.560
You will get better. The
body needs challenges.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.030
Without challenges, that's how
autoimmune problems happen.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.640
Medical shows are fun, but
medical games are even more fun.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.530
Check out this video of me
playing Surgeon Simulator.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:25.320
I make a really good surgeon.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.450
Not accurate, but very fun to watch.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.634
Click here, check it out. As
always, stay happy and healthy.

00:08:30.634 --> 00:08:32.036
(whispering) Click it.

00:08:32.036 --> 00:08:34.740
Operation.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.323
(upbeat music)

